# 16 Conclusion

This investigation compared how three leading Large Language Models—ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini—approached the same product discovery and root cause analysis task under identical testing conditions.

Although all three models successfully followed the general objective of investigating the problem before proposing solutions, each demonstrated a distinct reasoning strategy.

ChatGPT produced the most comprehensive investigation, combining strong business thinking, structured hypothesis generation, and a detailed investigation roadmap.

Claude demonstrated the highest level of evidence discipline, consistently distinguishing observations, assumptions, and hypotheses while carefully managing uncertainty throughout the investigation.

Gemini delivered the clearest executive-style investigation, emphasizing organization, prioritization, and practical communication for business stakeholders.

The evaluation showed that differences between the models were driven less by overall capability than by their preferred reasoning strategies.

This finding suggests that selecting an LLM for business investigations should depend on the analytical approach required rather than assuming that one model is universally superior.

More broadly, this project demonstrates that evaluating AI systems for business applications requires more than measuring factual accuracy.

Meaningful evaluation should also consider:

- problem framing;
- evidence discipline;
- clarification strategy;
- hypothesis generation;
- investigation design;
- decision support.

These characteristics determine how effectively an AI system can assist product teams when investigating complex business problems with incomplete information.

Ultimately, this investigation shows that Large Language Models can serve as valuable partners in product discovery, provided their reasoning processes are understood, critically evaluated, and applied appropriately to the business context.


